<?xml version="1.0"?>
<Definitions xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
	<EntityComponents>

<!-- Spawn Conditions for basic installations -->
	<EntityComponent xsi:type="MyObjectBuilder_InventoryComponentDefinition">
		<Id>
			<TypeId>Inventory</TypeId>
			<SubtypeId>WW2-SpawnConditions-Installations</SubtypeId>
		</Id>
		<Description>
			[MES Spawn Conditions]

			[PlanetaryInstallation:true]
			[RivalAiPlanetaryInstallation:true]

			[InstallationSpawnsOnDryLand:true]

			[MaxSpawnFromPlanetSurface:5000]
			[PlanetRequiresAtmo:true]
			[UsePlayerCountCheck:true]
			[MinimumPlayers:1]
			[IgnoreCombatPhase:true]

			[CutVoxelsAtAirtightCells:true]
			[CutVoxelSize:2.7]

		</Description>
	</EntityComponent>

	<!-- Shallow-water wrecks (Golo hulks, etc.): spawns in/against water instead of dry land.
	     Correction 2026-09-12: MinWaterDepth is NOT dead. It parses into a field via
	     SpawnConditionsProfile.cs, and a coarse gate in SpawnConditions.cs is commented out, but
	     the real enforcement for PlanetaryInstallation water spawns lives in PathPlacements.cs
	     (CalculatePlanetaryInstallationCoords / IsPositionSurfaceValid), which actively rejects
	     positions shallower than MinWaterDepth for UnderWaterStation/WaterSurfaceStation types.
	     MinWaterDepth:1 below is genuinely working. There is still no MaxWaterDepth anywhere in
	     MES (checked PathPlacements.cs too, not just SpawnConditions.cs) - a PR adding one is in
	     progress with MeridiusIX.
	     MinWaterCoverage/MaxWaterCoverage are a separate, complementary signal, not a substitute
	     for the (working) depth check: checked against environment.WaterInSurroundingAreaRatio,
	     the fraction of water found sampling 8 compass directions x 12 rings spaced 1000m apart
	     (out to 11km) from the spawn point - a regional coastal-vs-open-sea classifier, not depth
	     at the exact point. MES's own code comment claims true open ocean never exceeds ~0.6 on
	     their test planet, but that ceiling is plausibly tied to THEIR planet's radius, not a
	     universal constant - the sample directions are computed once at the spawn point and offset
	     in a straight line rather than following the sphere, so on a smaller planet (ours is ~30km
	     radius, see WW2-Reference-PlanetCreation.md) the outer rings drift further off the
	     intended compass bearing before being reprojected onto the terrain.
	     Measured 2026-09-12 via /MES.Info.GetDiagnostics: a harbor mouth (Valencia) read 0.438,
	     good open-coast spots read up to 0.59, and open ocean between the Balearics and Sardinia
	     read a clean 1.0 - confirms the "never above 0.6" claim above is planet-specific to MES's
	     own test planet, not universal (this one clearly goes to 1.0). MaxWaterCoverage:0.42 is set
	     just above the measured harbor-mouth reading and comfortably below the 0.59 good-coast
	     spots, keeping wrecks in genuinely close-to-shore water without reaching out toward
	     deeper/further-out spots - though pushing this up toward 0.55-0.6 later could be a
	     deliberate way to get a second, more "lost at sea" wreck variant instead of just coastal
	     ones. If wrecks still turn up somewhere wrong once MinWaterDepth's real effect is accounted
	     for, switch these to hand-placed [StaticEncounter] spots instead, same as the Ports.

	     2026-09-14: the La Malouine wreck test spawn above landed in a spot ~280m deep - exactly the
	     "no MaxWaterDepth exists" gap called out since 2026-09-12. Per the user, Enenra (MES dev
	     team) is adding a real [MaxWaterDepth:float] SpawnCondition in an upcoming update. A note for
	     that value (7.5) is left as plain text right before [MinWaterCoverage:0.15] below, NOT a real
	     [MaxWaterDepth:7.5] tag and deliberately NOT inside an XML comment either - restore it as a
	     real tag once Enenra's update ships.
	     Correction, same day: an earlier version of this fix wrapped that placeholder in an XML
	     comment tag, nested directly inside the Description element below - that broke mod loading
	     entirely (confirmed from the real SpaceEngineers.log, not just guessed):
	     SE's generated XmlSerializer reads Description via ReadElementString(), which throws
	     System.Xml.XmlException: "Unexpected node type Comment" on ANY child node in that element,
	     comments included - it's not a "comments are always safely stripped" situation like normal
	     XML parsing, at least not for this specific reader. The MOD_CRITICAL_ERROR that reported
	     SpawnGroups-Wrecks.sbc as the failing file was a red herring - the log's actual exception
	     traced to this file, one file earlier in the load batch; SE's crit-error attribution can name
	     the file to load work moves to.
	     7.5m applies to this profile since it currently covers all of the Utility/Corvette/Destroyer
	     wrecks (Golo, Gabbiano/La Malouine, the four destroyer hulls). The user also specified 12m
	     for "the rest" (Cruiser/Heavy Cruiser/Carrier/Battleship-tier wrecks) - no such wrecks exist
	     yet, so there's nothing to attach that value to; when a larger-hull wreck is eventually built,
	     give it its own SpawnConditions profile (same shallow-water InstallationSpawnsUnderwater
	     setup as this one) with a real [MaxWaterDepth:12] tag instead of reusing this profile.

	     2026-09-13: added [AlignToSurface:true], a brand-new MES tag (Update 2.74.02 patch notes: "for
	     planetary installations, overriding the default of aligning to gravity").
	     Correction, same day: the initial version of this comment claimed it "tilts the spawned grid
	     to match the local surface normal" - that was inferred from the patch note wording alone and
	     was wrong to state with confidence. Checked the actual GitHub master source directly:
	     SpawnGroupManager.cs:907 is the ONLY place AlignToSurface is referenced anywhere in the MES
	     codebase, and it only COPIES the flag from PlanetaryInstallationSettings into the
	     SpawnConditionsProfile - nothing anywhere (PathPlacements.cs included) ever reads that field
	     back out to actually change the spawn orientation. As shipped right now, this tag appears to
	     be inert - present in the tag parser but not yet wired to real behavior, possibly a stub ahead
	     of its consuming logic. Left in place since it's harmless and free once MES does wire it up,
	     but don't rely on it doing anything today, and don't cite it as an explanation for orientation
	     or embedding behavior seen in-game.

	     2026-09-13: given the above, planetary installations here are still oriented via
	     PathPlacements.cs's own default (UpAtPosition, i.e. straight radial/gravity-up from planet
	     center) regardless of this tag - grids sit level, not slope-tilted, same as before. -->
	<EntityComponent xsi:type="MyObjectBuilder_InventoryComponentDefinition">
		<Id>
			<TypeId>Inventory</TypeId>
			<SubtypeId>WW2-SpawnConditions-Wreck-ShallowWater</SubtypeId>
		</Id>
		<Description>
			[MES Spawn Conditions]

			[PlanetaryInstallation:true]
			[RivalAiPlanetaryInstallation:true]

			[InstallationSpawnsOnDryLand:false]
			[InstallationSpawnsUnderwater:true]
			[MinWaterDepth:1]
			PLACEHOLDER MaxWaterDepth:7.5 disabled - not live in MES yet, see comment above; restore as a real [MaxWaterDepth:7.5] tag once Enenra's update ships
			[MinWaterCoverage:0.15]
			[MaxWaterCoverage:0.42]
			[AlignToSurface:true]

			[MaxSpawnFromPlanetSurface:5000]
			[PlanetRequiresAtmo:true]
			[UsePlayerCountCheck:true]
			[MinimumPlayers:1]
			[IgnoreCombatPhase:true]

			[CutVoxelsAtAirtightCells:true]
			[CutVoxelSize:2.7]

		</Description>
	</EntityComponent>

	</EntityComponents>
</Definitions>

